  "name": "Question 27",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the {{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero.",
  "worked_solution": "To check whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(-2x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ we have that $f$ is even.\n\nFor the Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is even and $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(nx)$ is odd, so $f(x)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(nx)$ is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${b}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{even}$ and the $\\text{sine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
